  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "63cd9821-f48b-4d86-9bd8-b7250433aa53",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** The goal is to help students recognize that Web Apps are interactive systems defined by their structure and response, regardless of how they are accessed. It shifts their focus from \"software icons\" to the design patterns (like menus and buttons) that make those apps usable across different devices.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a moment to jot down their responses. Have students share with a partner and then call on a few volunteers to share with the class. As students respond, write key verbs on the board (e.g., message, buy, upload, search, post, edit).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for students mentioning specific actions like \"ordering food,\" \"checking grades,\" or messaging friends\" rather than app names. If students get stuck, prompt with: \"Could you still search? Still message? Still buy something?\"",
        "name": "The App-Less Day"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "63cd9821-f48b-4d86-9bd8-b7250433aa53",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4accc299-4602-4063-a00c-00f3af9a0199"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f00e68e8-1d0d-4d43-94c3-e7fb5d80dc35",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Notice that even without installing an app, you still expect to message, search, post, or buy. That's because what matters isn't the icon, it's the interaction.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students focus on the app itself, redirect with: \"What are you actually doing in that app?\" or \"What action are you trying to complete.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f00e68e8-1d0d-4d43-94c3-e7fb5d80dc35",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4accc299-4602-4063-a00c-00f3af9a0199"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b1b55d2-9ccf-4ff7-97d2-d9c5b7c47e48",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b1b55d2-9ccf-4ff7-97d2-d9c5b7c47e48",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4accc299-4602-4063-a00c-00f3af9a0199"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0c60f558-06ea-4673-8299-631fd23739d4",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Foundations of AI Programming (JavaScript) Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define web app. Emphasize the word \"respond\" and connect it to the verbs students just shared.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0c60f558-06ea-4673-8299-631fd23739d4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4accc299-4602-4063-a00c-00f3af9a0199"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a4b41de-7725-4845-9dcf-651793f5c317",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Notice you didn't say \"open Instagram.\" You said things like message, search, and post. A web app isn't just information. It responds to your actions. Today, we're going to look at how those responses are intentionally designed and how web apps affect our lives.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a4b41de-7725-4845-9dcf-651793f5c317",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4accc299-4602-4063-a00c-00f3af9a0199"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7a6b0632-e70b-463f-939c-95f606edaa7e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7a6b0632-e70b-463f-939c-95f606edaa7e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4accc299-4602-4063-a00c-00f3af9a0199"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "78ee248f-7233-4803-98a9-588ed29f716e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students discover that web pages are intentionally structured and that visible parts work together to support user interaction.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of two.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a set of the mystery components and a blank sheet of paper.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students choose one app purpose.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students arrange the mystery components on a blank sheet of paper to create an app layout. They should:\n1. Arrange all components on their paper.\n2. Write on the components to show what they are – for example, if a component has a button, what is the name of that button? If there is a heading, what does it say?\n3. For every button or interactive element, draw an arrow and write what the app does in response – for example, opens the menu or shows a dog's bio in a pop-up window.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Pair each group with another pair. Instruct one group to act as users while the other acts as their app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for whether students are thinking in actions and responses. Listen for moments where a tester says, \"What happens when I click this?\" If an app has no clear response, prompt designers to add one. Encourage students to think in terms of: User action → App response.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Paper App Prototype",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If groups finish early, ask: \"Did the app actually help you complete the goal?\", \"Where did you get stuck?\", or \"What did you expect to happen that didn't?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "78ee248f-7233-4803-98a9-588ed29f716e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d7cec99b-f728-4741-a4bc-f2d3ab10f093"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a30b325-6baf-43ca-8dc2-e3b927c456ec",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a30b325-6baf-43ca-8dc2-e3b927c456ec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d7cec99b-f728-4741-a4bc-f2d3ab10f093"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "21b04ee5-c2ec-4bdb-b705-cdb9786d26a5",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have pairs first meet with a group that chose the same app and discuss the questions in their activity guide. Then have them meet with a group that chose a different app and discuss the same questions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As you walk around, listen for students noticing similar component placement, especially elements placed at the top or grouped together. Pay attention to moments where a user taps something and the app doesn't respond. If groups get stuck, prompt them by asking what surprised them, whether they noticed anything showing up in similar locations, or what happened when someone tried to use the layout.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is limited, prioritize the same-app comparison — that's where pattern recognition is strongest and the \"design is intentional\" insight usually emerges."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "21b04ee5-c2ec-4bdb-b705-cdb9786d26a5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d7cec99b-f728-4741-a4bc-f2d3ab10f093"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6276cfd2-4137-45d3-b096-180844bb6dbd",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6276cfd2-4137-45d3-b096-180844bb6dbd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d7cec99b-f728-4741-a4bc-f2d3ab10f093"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76f3f2f7-d7af-431e-afbc-e61d6255c45a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have pairs meet with a group that chose a different app and discuss the same questions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What did you notice about where most groups placed their components?*\n- *Why do you think so many layouts looked similar?*\n- *When testing, what happened if a user clicked something the app hadn't planned for?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize that their layouts were not random. Even without instructions, many groups placed components in similar locations and expected certain parts to respond when clicked. Students should begin to realize that web app structure follows shared design patterns and that interaction only works when those parts are intentionally planned.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76f3f2f7-d7af-431e-afbc-e61d6255c45a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d7cec99b-f728-4741-a4bc-f2d3ab10f093"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5afbbb72-363a-4159-9332-c2d77b69e2cd",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Introduce pattern recognition and common components that make up web apps.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Common App Components"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5afbbb72-363a-4159-9332-c2d77b69e2cd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d7cec99b-f728-4741-a4bc-f2d3ab10f093"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd7cc91b-8cd5-45a4-a725-97081a88090d",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "What you just noticed has a name. In design and in computer science, we call this pattern recognition — spotting recurring structures that tell us something about how a system works. You weren't given instructions about where to put the navigation bar. You just knew. That's because you've seen it in the same place hundreds of times, and your brain learned the pattern. Designers use this skill when they study what works across different sites. And AI uses it too — it learns patterns from data the same way you learn patterns from experience. We'll come back to this idea throughout the unit.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd7cc91b-8cd5-45a4-a725-97081a88090d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d7cec99b-f728-4741-a4bc-f2d3ab10f093"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "814118e4-c409-4883-89db-369704b347d2",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define pattern recognition. Point to the three layouts on the slide and ask students to identify what matched across all of them.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "814118e4-c409-4883-89db-369704b347d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d7cec99b-f728-4741-a4bc-f2d3ab10f093"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f293820-2f42-4232-a09a-46d02fe81e48",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Many of you placed this strip across the top in very similar ways. No one told you to do that. That's because you've already learned how web apps are structured just by using them. You've been thinking like designers — even if you didn't know the technical terms yet.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f293820-2f42-4232-a09a-46d02fe81e48",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d7cec99b-f728-4741-a4bc-f2d3ab10f093"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a3ab718b-c282-4a6b-b427-dcbf4b9ff36a",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click to reveal the first component name: Navigation Bar. Point to where it appeared in student prototypes and on the example page. Explain that it is the menu across the top of a webpage that helps users move between pages or sections and is often referred to as a nav bar.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click to reveal the next component name: Hero Section. Point to where it appeared in student prototypes and on the example page. Explain that it is the large banner near the top that introduces the main idea, with a heading, short text, and usually a \"call to action\" button.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click to reveal the next component name: Gallery Cards. Point to where it appeared in student prototypes and on the example page. Explain that these are the boxes that group related information, each with a picture, title, and short description.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click to reveal the final component name: Footer. Point to where it appeared in student prototypes and on the example page. Explain that this is the strip at the bottom that often includes contact info or copyright details.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 1 to match the web app component to its description.",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "This level gives an immediate read on whether students can match and apply the four component names.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a3ab718b-c282-4a6b-b427-dcbf4b9ff36a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d7cec99b-f728-4741-a4bc-f2d3ab10f093"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e41ddf1-4823-45de-9293-3582c01f694e",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e41ddf1-4823-45de-9293-3582c01f694e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d7cec99b-f728-4741-a4bc-f2d3ab10f093"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b5f74ed9-e02f-4fdc-a710-579cac99dea6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students respond to the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that web apps shape our daily lives because they are interactive systems built around shared design patterns. These patterns allow us to quickly understand how to navigate, search, buy, post, and communicate. Students should understand that web apps are intentionally structured by designers to support specific actions and responses — and that this structure influences how we think, move, and make decisions online.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "As students share, reinforce these connections explicitly:\n- Web apps shape our lives because they respond to what we do — they aren't just pages, they are systems.\n- We navigate quickly because designers use shared layout patterns (like navigation at the top and grouped content).\n- When structure changes, our experience changes.\n- Behind every web app is a team of humans making decisions about how it should look, feel, and function"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b5f74ed9-e02f-4fdc-a710-579cac99dea6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "030e4cd6-6b8a-4dad-bc3d-cf8798368385"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dbfd92b7-8f76-4662-9092-db3ef4d3dfe4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dbfd92b7-8f76-4662-9092-db3ef4d3dfe4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "030e4cd6-6b8a-4dad-bc3d-cf8798368385"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d12d5616-1fc9-4fca-a3fa-d5a6bb900ea5",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Think about an app you use every day. Who do you think made the decisions about how it looks and works?*\n- *What would you want to know about how that happened?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** This is a baseline prompt, not a comprehension check. Students don't need to get the \"right\" answer. Listen for what they already believe: some may say developers, some may say the company, some may say AI. Surface those ideas without correcting them. The goal is to capture where students are starting from — especially any assumptions that AI makes these decisions on its own — so those ideas can be revisited at the end of the unit.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d12d5616-1fc9-4fca-a3fa-d5a6bb900ea5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "030e4cd6-6b8a-4dad-bc3d-cf8798368385"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3808ffe6-9bd2-4f70-b96c-90b2804e3d6c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students use their knowledge of common web page components to predict what an AI-generated webpage will include and begin to recognize that AI generates outputs based on familiar design patterns."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3808ffe6-9bd2-4f70-b96c-90b2804e3d6c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "093e12ed-da61-4c56-92ed-f5b2fec9b112"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93152a9c-bbf1-4742-986a-b0cb62fd82b0",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This is an example of a prompt — it's what someone types into an AI tool to tell it what to create.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93152a9c-bbf1-4742-986a-b0cb62fd82b0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "093e12ed-da61-4c56-92ed-f5b2fec9b112"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f2bc73b2-cca2-4245-9557-44e602ae0c2b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What sections will likely appear?*\n- *What components do you expect?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students draw on their experience with common web page components (e.g., navigation bar, hero section, cards, footer) to predict what the AI-generated page will include. Listen for students identifying familiar structures and elements and explaining their reasoning based on patterns they've seen across websites. Students should begin to recognize that AI generates predictable layouts because it follows common design and language patterns.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students compare their predictions from the previous slide to the AI-generated web page shown. Ask a few students to share what matched their predictions, focusing on components such as navigation bars, hero sections, and content sections. Guide students to recognize that the layout and structure feel familiar because AI follows common design patterns, not because it truly understands the content. Then transition by telling students you’ll take a closer look at how AI generates these kinds of results.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep this moment brief and focused. The goal is not to analyze every detail, but to help students recognize that AI-generated web pages follow predictable patterns based on what they’ve seen before."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f2bc73b2-cca2-4245-9557-44e602ae0c2b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "093e12ed-da61-4c56-92ed-f5b2fec9b112"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "67ddf038-4574-4dc5-a7f8-b2b718f11da6",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "67ddf038-4574-4dc5-a7f8-b2b718f11da6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "093e12ed-da61-4c56-92ed-f5b2fec9b112"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fd87bfb8-71c4-4cee-b4cd-e4c5a13d9314",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fd87bfb8-71c4-4cee-b4cd-e4c5a13d9314",
        "lesson_activity.key": "093e12ed-da61-4c56-92ed-f5b2fec9b112"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a9a18b93-6a75-4048-8f2a-3918c28df0ab",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand that AI generates responses by recognizing patterns from existing examples and that what they include in a prompt influences the result.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that AI doesn't understand code the way a human does. Instead, it predicts what to generate based on patterns it has seen in many examples. Emphasize that what students include in their prompt will influence what the AI produces. This is why the same prompt often produces similar types of web pages.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students' attention to the collection of web app examples feeding into the brain graphic. Explain that these represent the training data the AI learned from before anyone ever typed a prompt. Clarify that training data consists of real web apps created by humans and that the AI studied many examples to learn from them. Emphasize that the AI does not begin with built-in knowledge about restaurants or web design, it learns from large collections of human-created content.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point to the repeated web app components shown on the slide and explain that the AI looks across many examples to identify what appears frequently. Highlight specific repeated features such as navigation bars at the top, red call-to-action buttons, large headlines, and menu sections with prices. Introduce the term \"patterns\" as structures or design choices that show up again and again across many sites. Reinforce that this step is about noticing similarities, not generating anything yet.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students' attention to the brain-to-website diagram and explain that this step represents generation. Clarify that after identifying patterns, the AI produces new content by predicting what usually comes next. Connect this back to the web page students saw earlier to show how those predictions result in the final layout.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain how AI uses pattern recognition and that students use this same skill to analyze layouts, debug code, and spot effectiveness.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "How AI Makes Predictions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a9a18b93-6a75-4048-8f2a-3918c28df0ab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a512f242-5924-428d-9f76-b2ad1fd0259a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "819fb242-1b1d-40ce-a9e0-5439e4a91722",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students explore the Web Lab tool and begin using AI to generate and modify their webpage, recognizing how their prompts influence the results.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students get out their copy of the Foundations of AI Programming (JavaScript) Unit Guide.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Explore Web Lab and Prompting"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "819fb242-1b1d-40ce-a9e0-5439e4a91722",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a512f242-5924-428d-9f76-b2ad1fd0259a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "36d7f77d-3f87-49ee-b46f-38da0b3c1c50",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You just saw how AI generates web pages by recognizing patterns from examples. Designers often use AI tools to quickly create and test ideas like this. Now you're going to explore a tool that can generate and change your webpage for you, and start experimenting with how it works. Play around with the tool and see what you can discover. Don't worry! You can't break anything!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "36d7f77d-3f87-49ee-b46f-38da0b3c1c50",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a512f242-5924-428d-9f76-b2ad1fd0259a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1326013-ed0e-4209-ac20-2d14ec94c822",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 1 to explore the tool and select different tabs and buttons to see what they can discover.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Encourage students to select different tabs on the left-hand side, switch between Code and Preview, and try asking the AI to make a simple change to the page. Remind students not to worry about making mistakes — they can't break anything.",
        "progression_name": "Explore Web Lab",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Once students begin to slow down in their exploration, move the class into the discussion to capture what they discovered."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1326013-ed0e-4209-ac20-2d14ec94c822",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a512f242-5924-428d-9f76-b2ad1fd0259a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2f71e756-3e58-4333-821f-ad730a3b9d16",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2f71e756-3e58-4333-821f-ad730a3b9d16",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a512f242-5924-428d-9f76-b2ad1fd0259a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b8b803d8-553a-4668-9e88-a90c5901678a",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts. Invite students to share what they discovered while exploring the tool. As students respond, briefly highlight the features they mention so all students are aware of them. If students raise questions about specific features, demonstrate them quickly without going into detail. Make sure to point out the different views, the file panel with different files and folders, and the tabs along the left-hand side which include the AI Tutor, Version History, and Backpack.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep this discussion focused and fast. The goal is to orient students to key features of Web Lab, not to explain how everything works."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b8b803d8-553a-4668-9e88-a90c5901678a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a512f242-5924-428d-9f76-b2ad1fd0259a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "87530c9b-19f1-4b5a-a3d9-1f10f521bd9b",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "87530c9b-19f1-4b5a-a3d9-1f10f521bd9b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a512f242-5924-428d-9f76-b2ad1fd0259a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ddea648d-b65b-40f9-b3b0-a614171e836c",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As you were exploring, you may have noticed that some AI changes affected the content of the page, while others changed the way it looked. Let’s take a closer look at that difference.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ddea648d-b65b-40f9-b3b0-a614171e836c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a512f242-5924-428d-9f76-b2ad1fd0259a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "27533af2-2332-4dd3-ab9c-54c1fa34b5ff",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to point out that AI generates separate files for each HTML page and a CSS file.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students what differences they notice between the two versions of the webpage shown. Guide them to identify what parts seem to control the structure and content versus what changes the visual appearance. Clarify that HTML provides the structure and content of a page, while CSS controls the style and visual design. Emphasize that students will work as Web Designers and use AI tools to make changes to both structure and style.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to share a few observations before you confirm or clarify. If needed, use simple analogies such as HTML being the structure or blueprint of the page and CSS being the visual design or style. Keep the focus on noticing differences rather than explaining code."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "27533af2-2332-4dd3-ab9c-54c1fa34b5ff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a512f242-5924-428d-9f76-b2ad1fd0259a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef9184cd-0a54-488f-8b9a-6ecfb471666c",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef9184cd-0a54-488f-8b9a-6ecfb471666c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a512f242-5924-428d-9f76-b2ad1fd0259a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "08ffc0ad-3a39-4318-bb7e-637963143fd8",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *prompt engineering*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that AI uses the words in a prompt to predict a response based on patterns it has learned. Point out that the AI pays attention to the task, context, tone, and details in a prompt, but keep the explanation high-level. Emphasize that what students include in their prompt will directly affect what the AI generates.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students what they think the AI-generated webpage might look like from this prompt. Guide students to notice that the prompt is vague, so the AI has to make guesses about what to include.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students how the AI-generated webpage might be different with this prompt. Invite students to compare it to the previous example and identify what would likely improve. Guide students to notice that adding context and specific details helps the AI generate a more organized and useful result."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "08ffc0ad-3a39-4318-bb7e-637963143fd8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a512f242-5924-428d-9f76-b2ad1fd0259a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9d7a4637-5cec-4507-bd0b-c17bbedd29af",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students use prompt engineering to generate their first AI-assisted webpage, discovering that the clarity and specificity of their instructions directly shapes the quality of what AI produces.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 2 to complete Levels 2 and 3.\n- **Level 2:** Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n- **Level 3:** Students complete as many AI Design Challenges as they can.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Move around the room and check that students are using AI to make changes to their page rather than editing code directly. Look for students trying different prompts, reviewing the results, and refining their page over time. Ask questions like, \"What are you trying to change?\" and \"Did the AI output match what you expected?\" Encourage students to preview their page and make adjustments based on what they see.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "AI Design Challenges",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9d7a4637-5cec-4507-bd0b-c17bbedd29af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a512f242-5924-428d-9f76-b2ad1fd0259a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ffb64746-6661-41cf-ba96-2ab496665e0f",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "AI Design Challenges",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "This level can be used as a formative assessment of the lesson objectives, including generating a web page using AI, refining prompts to improve results, and using iterative testing to make changes to HTML and CSS."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to keep moving between challenges rather than trying to perfect one change. If students get stuck, prompt them to try a different or more specific request to the AI instead of troubleshooting on their own."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ffb64746-6661-41cf-ba96-2ab496665e0f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a512f242-5924-428d-9f76-b2ad1fd0259a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ee3721e0-a598-4bdd-a5cb-138f84d967b8",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ee3721e0-a598-4bdd-a5cb-138f84d967b8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a512f242-5924-428d-9f76-b2ad1fd0259a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fca08529-0258-4c39-8050-7fb467fe0e80",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *Why might AI sometimes misunderstand what we mean?*\n- *How did you decide whether to accept or change the AI's output?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on their experience using AI and recognize that unclear or vague prompts can lead to unexpected results. Listen for students describing how they adjusted what they asked the AI to improve their webpage and how they decided whether to accept or change the output. Students should begin to understand that working with AI involves both giving clear instructions and making decisions about the results.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Validate student ideas and connect responses to their experience in the activity. Highlight that students improved their results by adjusting what they asked the AI and deciding whether to accept or revise the output. Reinforce that they are responsible for guiding and refining what the AI produces."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fca08529-0258-4c39-8050-7fb467fe0e80",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a512f242-5924-428d-9f76-b2ad1fd0259a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bb66675d-5d32-4453-a361-fc82ef00b9f9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to draw on what they noticed while prompting the AI, specifically how it generated their web page from prompts but often needed human refinement or direction. After a minute, invite a few volunteers to share highlights with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *AI learns from web apps that already exist. What kinds of designs do you think it reproduces most often?*\n- *What kinds might it get wrong?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should begin to notice that if AI learns from existing websites, it will reproduce the most common design patterns most confidently — and may struggle with anything underrepresented in that data, such as designs for users with accessibility needs, non-Western layout conventions, or newer design styles. Students don't need to arrive at a complete answer here. The goal is to plant the question so it has context when it resurfaces in upcoming lessons.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bb66675d-5d32-4453-a361-fc82ef00b9f9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d829101d-ab4a-4f31-b4b0-e69f05f3e5cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "51a10a1f-fb38-4f0e-8a03-a752268c29bb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students quickly recall the difference between HTML and CSS by visualizing what happens when styling is removed. They connect this idea to brand clarity and user experience — realizing that CSS gives identity and consistency to a site.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Recall that web apps are made with HTML and CSS and their roles.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show a webpage styled with CSS. Pose the question: \"If we removed the CSS, what would be left?\" Students sketch a quick version of the same page without CSS and annotate their sketches with reasoning (e.g., \"Text still shows but without color,\" \"Images stack vertically,\" \"No clear brand identity\"). After sketching, have a few students share what they noticed. Encourage students to connect their observations to how CSS communicates tone and brand consistency.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for misconceptions like \"nothing will show.\" Prompt with: \"Would the content still exist without CSS?\" or \"What happens to layout and colors if CSS is gone?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does CSS turn a brand kit or design idea into a real web page?*\n- *What do designers need to communicate clearly when styling a page?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should connect their understanding of CSS to real-world design workflows. The goal is to get students to recognize that CSS isn't just about color or font — it's about translating design decisions into code and ensuring clear communication when turning designs into webpages."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "51a10a1f-fb38-4f0e-8a03-a752268c29bb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "67e10d37-bb2d-475a-b0f1-81418938d9f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "46d44bdc-9ecc-4de5-85d5-d5fb39a05ea4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When a designer creates a layout or brand kit, it's not automatically ready for the web. CSS is how those visual design decisions are brought to life on a webpage — translating colors, spacing, and typography into a consistent look and feel.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "46d44bdc-9ecc-4de5-85d5-d5fb39a05ea4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "67e10d37-bb2d-475a-b0f1-81418938d9f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "941ae881-8e7c-4342-80f4-b9f0ece1f278",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "941ae881-8e7c-4342-80f4-b9f0ece1f278",
        "lesson_activity.key": "67e10d37-bb2d-475a-b0f1-81418938d9f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d4f4f252-a052-42a1-b2aa-6c09ad9a9d4d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal of the Activity: Establish students' role as front-end developers who translate brand kits into live, coded web pages using CSS.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that students will act as front-end developers using AI as their coding partner.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Stylesheets",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Refer back to this role throughout the activity. Remind students they're testing and refining AI-generated CSS like professionals validating a build. Remind students that AI can generate CSS syntax quickly, but it does not understand their specific brand or user needs. Their job is to define the intent and evaluate whether the output reflects it."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d4f4f252-a052-42a1-b2aa-6c09ad9a9d4d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "294ba5fa-f4f8-4583-8ab7-5b452952e33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c8d62147-3798-4628-9e36-0a4f587119b1",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c8d62147-3798-4628-9e36-0a4f587119b1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "294ba5fa-f4f8-4583-8ab7-5b452952e33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4eff0ac1-e480-459d-bdc8-5db6659bdbd8",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Foundations of AI Programming (JavaScript) Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *stylesheet*. Explain that CSS is the bridge between design and development: it's how the decisions made in a brand kit — like colors, fonts, and layout — become consistent code. Every time students see a website where the colors, fonts, and spacing look intentional and consistent, that's CSS at work.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the three requirements that students need to include when writing AI prompts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which AI prompt is better? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify that Prompt #2 is better because it clearly defines the goal for the styling. They should recognize that Prompt #1 is too vague (\"look modern and professional\"), and Prompt #2 gives a specific styling goal (colors, fonts, and which parts of the page to style). Help students see the difference between a vague goal → unclear results and a specific goal → more predictable, accurate results.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which AI prompt is better? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify that Prompt #2 is better because it provides clear, specific constraints like a dark color scheme and simple font style. They should recognize that \"nice\" and \"easy to read\" in Prompt #1 are subjective, and AI may interpret those differently than the student intended. Help students understand that constraints = clear rules AI must follow, and more specific constraints lead to more predictable and consistent results.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which AI prompt is better? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify that Prompt #2 is better because it defines specific problems to avoid in the design. They should recognize that Prompt #1 is vague and doesn't prevent issues, and Prompt #2 anticipates what could go wrong (contrast, readability, spacing). Help students understand that edge cases = what to avoid, and strong prompts don't just say what to do — they also say what should not happen.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the three questions in `prompt.md`. Explain that these are the same questions a Front-End Developer asks before starting any design task."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4eff0ac1-e480-459d-bdc8-5db6659bdbd8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "294ba5fa-f4f8-4583-8ab7-5b452952e33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bf96c014-ba39-4ee4-9f60-2898d991fda1",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The goal tells the AI what you're going for — the look, the feel, the vibe. The constraints are the rules it has to follow, like staying within your existing HTML or keeping certain colors. And edge cases are the things that could go wrong — like text that's too long, or a layout that breaks on a small screen. When you answer all three, your prompt becomes a plan, not just a request.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bf96c014-ba39-4ee4-9f60-2898d991fda1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "294ba5fa-f4f8-4583-8ab7-5b452952e33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "35a61c84-4695-4a61-86bf-db64dac70f1d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that .md stands for \"markdown\" — a simple way to format plain text. Markdown files don't require special software to open or edit, which is why developers use them everywhere. They live inside the project folder, so they travel with the code and stay accessible to everyone working on the project.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the example `prompt.md` on screen."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "35a61c84-4695-4a61-86bf-db64dac70f1d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "294ba5fa-f4f8-4583-8ab7-5b452952e33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2f0ac7dc-d1d4-4abc-9061-f74903136f71",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Once you've filled this in, you don't write a prompt from scratch. You give this file directly to the AI and let it do the work. The planning IS the prompt.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2f0ac7dc-d1d4-4abc-9061-f74903136f71",
        "lesson_activity.key": "294ba5fa-f4f8-4583-8ab7-5b452952e33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7534dfa9-d078-4421-9ed4-2d2a07f71d0c",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 3.\n- **Level 1:** Students read about Front-End Developers.\n- **Level 2:** Students draft a structured prompt using goal, constraints, and edge cases before prompting AI.\n- **Level 3:** Students use their plan to prompt AI to apply styles based on their plan.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students planned their prompt on Level 2 and use that to generate styles on Level 3.",
        "progression_name": "Front-End Developers"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7534dfa9-d078-4421-9ed4-2d2a07f71d0c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "294ba5fa-f4f8-4583-8ab7-5b452952e33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a7d9237-08c8-4000-884b-5053a558d10b",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Apply and Test a Stylesheet",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to \"debug like a designer.\" If the AI's output doesn't look right, they should describe why instead of just retrying the prompt."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a7d9237-08c8-4000-884b-5053a558d10b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "294ba5fa-f4f8-4583-8ab7-5b452952e33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50500256-c9db-40fe-b40d-f3e25e4d3fda",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50500256-c9db-40fe-b40d-f3e25e4d3fda",
        "lesson_activity.key": "294ba5fa-f4f8-4583-8ab7-5b452952e33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "07069aee-875b-4142-b643-45682a7f2cd8",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What did the AI change in your CSS — and why?*\n- *Did it make any styling choices you wouldn't have made yourself?*\n- *How do you know if its output matches your brand goals?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on how AI interprets prompts and makes automated styling decisions. They consider when AI-generated CSS aligns with their intent and when it diverges. The goal is for students to see themselves as responsible reviewers, not passive users — understanding that AI can enhance efficiency but still requires human judgment for accuracy, accessibility, and design intent."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "07069aee-875b-4142-b643-45682a7f2cd8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "294ba5fa-f4f8-4583-8ab7-5b452952e33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f3c05ae6-d356-42c0-a43e-4c0817ef7ace",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When designers use AI tools, they don’t assume every output is correct — they check what changed, why it changed, and whether it still fits their design goals.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f3c05ae6-d356-42c0-a43e-4c0817ef7ace",
        "lesson_activity.key": "294ba5fa-f4f8-4583-8ab7-5b452952e33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6bd28d03-b625-44a0-84a2-60430169e202",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal of the Activity: Students demonstrate mastery by refining and experimenting with CSS using AI assistance — improving accuracy, readability, and brand consistency.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 4 to complete Levels 4 and 5.\n- **Level 4:** Students complete at least one of the challenges.\n- **Level 5:** Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, check that they:\n- Clearly define what they are improving before prompting AI.\n- Make one change at a time and preview the results before layering additional edits.\n- Resize the preview to check how their page behaves on smaller screens and refine the CSS if text overlaps or sections stop fitting.\n- Document what changed and why it improved the design.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Style Choice Challenge",
        "progression_name": "Style Choice Challenge"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6bd28d03-b625-44a0-84a2-60430169e202",
        "lesson_activity.key": "294ba5fa-f4f8-4583-8ab7-5b452952e33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "656a72c6-36ff-4e91-a132-8d76947b9596",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 5 to formatively assess whether students can:\n- Describe one specific styling refinement they made.\n- Explain how they used AI intentionally (including how they adjusted their prompt, if needed).\n- Connect their change to improved brand alignment and usability.\n- Distinguish between structural changes (HTML) and styling changes (CSS)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "656a72c6-36ff-4e91-a132-8d76947b9596",
        "lesson_activity.key": "294ba5fa-f4f8-4583-8ab7-5b452952e33f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b7f99cb6-036d-41a3-8e9c-e87cb896fde2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b7f99cb6-036d-41a3-8e9c-e87cb896fde2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bfd86de1-43c7-4fcd-bccc-20b458acbe7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd0b554d-b3bb-43d8-8bce-d99a9b10c916",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students analyze how layout and visual organization affect a user’s ability to locate information and complete a task on a website.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What would you click first to start your pizza order?*\n- *What clues on the page tell you \"this is the ordering area\"?*\n- *What’s one thing that makes it harder to locate what you need?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice that the layout makes it difficult to quickly locate key actions or information. They may point out competing elements, unclear starting points, or labels that do not clearly guide the ordering process. Students should begin recognizing how visual hierarchy, grouping, and clear sections help users understand where to go next.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"Web Accessibility Perspectives: Clear Layout and Design\" video.\n\n**Questions to consider with the video:** \n- What made the first pizza site confusing?\n- What changed to make the improved version easier?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that clear layout and organization help users quickly understand where to start and what to do next. They should identify specific design changes—such as clearer headings and labels, navigation bar, and consistent styling—that make the improved site easier to navigate and use.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Where would you go first to start an order now?*\n- *What parts of the page are easier to locate?*\n- *What design choices help guide you through the steps?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice that the improved layout organizes the page into clear sections and highlights key actions. They may point out that steps like Find Store, Create Pizza, and Add Sides are grouped and labeled clearly, making it easier to understand where to begin and what to do next. Students should recognize that visual hierarchy, consistent layout, and grouping help users locate information quickly and complete tasks more easily."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd0b554d-b3bb-43d8-8bce-d99a9b10c916",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7a96bb7-333d-445d-ae01-50db53396238"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9493eea3-6baf-4f81-ad21-e3c076cf7ac9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI tools can generate layouts that look correct but still include usability issues. Designers need to review AI-generated designs and identify problems that could make a site harder to use.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9493eea3-6baf-4f81-ad21-e3c076cf7ac9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7a96bb7-333d-445d-ae01-50db53396238"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "52c90fc3-020c-4804-887a-41b008251c08",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What could go wrong if no one fixed these designs?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that poor design can prevent users from completing tasks, cause confusion or frustration, and lead users to abandon the site. They may mention difficulty finding information, making mistakes, or being unable to use the site at all. This reinforces that accessibility issues have real consequences and that designs—whether created by humans or AI—must be reviewed and improved to ensure they are usable.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students focus only on aesthetics (e.g., “it looks messy”), prompt them to consider the impact on the user: Can the user complete the task? What might cause them to give up? Connect this to AI by reminding students that AI-generated designs can include these same issues, so designers need to review and improve them."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "52c90fc3-020c-4804-887a-41b008251c08",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7a96bb7-333d-445d-ae01-50db53396238"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e90b1880-4daa-4c64-96a1-8ac43c32e9f5",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e90b1880-4daa-4c64-96a1-8ac43c32e9f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7a96bb7-333d-445d-ae01-50db53396238"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "201a8e95-395f-4384-b2d2-63b20e916f1f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "201a8e95-395f-4384-b2d2-63b20e916f1f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c7a96bb7-333d-445d-ae01-50db53396238"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "de6591d7-3780-47d9-82ff-cb338828d68d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students are introduced to the accessibility challenges that exist across the web and learn how those issues influence AI-generated designs. They explore the real-world role of the Inclusive Designer and understand why human judgment is essential in guiding AI toward more accessible outcomes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Foundations of AI Programming (JavaScript) Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the role of Inclusive Designers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that nearly 95% of websites have accessibility issues and highlight examples of common issues (low contrast text, missing alt text, unlabeled forms, empty links/buttons).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain how accessibility issues in web data can appear in AI-generated designs. Emphasize that AI learns patterns from existing websites, so issues in those sites can be reproduced in AI-generated outputs.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "How Accessible is the Web?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may assume AI \"knows better\" than humans. Emphasize that AI does not judge quality—it learns patterns from existing data, including flawed or inaccessible designs."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "de6591d7-3780-47d9-82ff-cb338828d68d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8841cdf-3b0a-4852-9a03-9e7959340964"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b8494c6-1789-455a-b65f-00dcfbebcc80",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b8494c6-1789-455a-b65f-00dcfbebcc80",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8841cdf-3b0a-4852-9a03-9e7959340964"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ee7013d2-5a76-4d66-9666-9da97ca13e07",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *accessibility*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 and 2.\n- **Level 1:** Students read about Inclusive Designers.\n- **Level 2:** Students explore one of the provided websites and identify potential usability or accessibility issues that could make it harder for a user to locate information or complete a task.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, prompt them with questions like:\n- \"What is the main task a user might want to complete on this page?\"\n- \"Is it easy to tell where to start?\"\n- \"Is anything hard to read, interact with, or understand?\"\n\nEncourage students to be specific about what part of the design causes the issue.",
        "progression_name": "Inclusive Designers"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ee7013d2-5a76-4d66-9666-9da97ca13e07",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8841cdf-3b0a-4852-9a03-9e7959340964"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6996f42b-dabc-4a30-b868-73a5f1757d7d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Accessibility Audit"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6996f42b-dabc-4a30-b868-73a5f1757d7d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8841cdf-3b0a-4852-9a03-9e7959340964"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "68828a52-dfa1-4655-84b8-79a1201541b3",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What accessibility issues did you find?*\n- *Were any of the issues surprising? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should surface specific, concrete issues from their audits rather than general observations — not just \"the contrast is low\" but \"this text would be unreadable for a user with low vision.\" For the second prompt, students may be surprised that well-known, prestigious institutions have accessibility failures. That surprise is worth naming: even experienced design teams miss accessibility issues. If multiple groups found the same issues across different sites, highlight that pattern — it suggests these are common, systemic failures rather than one-off mistakes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Recall that AI reproduces patterns from its training data, which means it will also reproduce accessibility issues it's been trained on. Reinforce that it's also important for human designers to watch for these patterns.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the POUR Principles as rules for making website accessible. Explain that each letter represents a type of issue designers use to evaluate websites: Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, and Robust."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "68828a52-dfa1-4655-84b8-79a1201541b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8841cdf-3b0a-4852-9a03-9e7959340964"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e5021f6c-e671-4018-ac92-5b728093166e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This is the framework Inclusive Designers use to evaluate whether a design is actually doing its job for all users.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e5021f6c-e671-4018-ac92-5b728093166e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8841cdf-3b0a-4852-9a03-9e7959340964"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f4ff673-4600-4e41-a278-b326b4cf66fb",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain what it means for something to be Perceivable on a web app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain what it means for something to be Operable on a web app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain what it means for something to be Understandable on a web app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain what it means for something to be Robust on a web app."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f4ff673-4600-4e41-a278-b326b4cf66fb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8841cdf-3b0a-4852-9a03-9e7959340964"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "85472c3a-800c-4b09-90cb-9fd6b478832a",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students learn how to use the POUR framework (Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust) to describe and organize accessibility issues they observed in AI-generated layouts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Accessibility in Design activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 through 5.\n- **Level 3:** Students identify at least two to three accessibility issues on the provided web app.\n- **Level 4:** Students apply accessibility principles to an AI-generated page with accessibility issues.\n- **Level 5:** Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Observe students as they identify accessibility issues and apply the POUR principles. Prompt them with questions like: “What POUR principle does this issue relate to?” or “How might this issue affect a user with this constraint?” Encourage students to be specific in their prompts, clearly describing the issue and referencing the relevant POUR principle. Push them to connect specific design elements (e.g., contrast, labels, button size) to real user challenges. Ensure students use the Accessibility Prompt Builder to generate and refine prompts that communicate issues clearly, rather than trying to fix everything at once.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "The POUR Principle",
        "progression_name": "Accessibility Issues and Fixes"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "85472c3a-800c-4b09-90cb-9fd6b478832a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8841cdf-3b0a-4852-9a03-9e7959340964"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b9b1f001-e7dc-47ba-bee8-2e67e7656ba8",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Levels 3 and 5 can serve as a formative assessment. Collect student responses to gauge their understanding of applying the POUR framework and user-centered design principles.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b9b1f001-e7dc-47ba-bee8-2e67e7656ba8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8841cdf-3b0a-4852-9a03-9e7959340964"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "245bc897-b730-452b-b110-39db928654c6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students respond to the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide. Give them time to write independently, then invite a few volunteers to share.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Whose needs were invisible in the AI's default output?*\n- *What does that tell us about who was — and wasn't — considered when that AI was trained?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should connect the accessibility failures they identified in the audit to decisions made during AI training — specifically, that AI reproduces the patterns it learned from, including patterns of exclusion. For the second question, students should recognize that when accessibility needs are underrepresented in training data, the resulting AI outputs will reflect that gap by default. Push students to be specific: not just \"disabled users weren't considered\" but naming a particular need they encountered in the audit and tracing it back to what the training data would have had to include for AI to handle it correctly.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "245bc897-b730-452b-b110-39db928654c6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5e167791-069c-46fa-a964-cb5f2c30c656"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b0c3464b-b5c5-46aa-b42c-40901741f5cf",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize that low-fidelity wireframes are easier to change than high-fidelity designs, and begin to understand why designers plan structure before building.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which is easier to change? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to notice that the wireframe on the right is easier to change because it focuses only on structure, not visual details. Students should understand that designers use \"low-fidelity\" sketches to quickly test and revise layout and user flow before adding visuals or code. This reinforces that planning structure is a human decision-making step that happens before AI or developers build the final product.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b0c3464b-b5c5-46aa-b42c-40901741f5cf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "54b2d521-768e-41e3-ac33-ec0b705b3986"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "514b6e31-2053-4b93-b0b7-47b8834f4913",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students begin creating low-fidelity wireframes by focusing on structure and layout, understanding that clear wireframes will be used to guide AI in building their webpage in the next lesson.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Foundations of AI Programming (JavaScript) Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *wireframe*. Use the blueprint analogy: just like an architect sketches a blueprint before building, designers create wireframes to plan structure and layout before adding visuals. Explain that this step keeps humans in the loop — designers decide structure and logic, while AI or developers handle styling and details later.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Wireframe Sketch Sprint ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Ask students what other kinds of jobs use \"blueprints\" or rough drafts before building the final version. This helps them connect the idea of planning structure first — a core design principle — to the broader concept of human decision-making before automation."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "514b6e31-2053-4b93-b0b7-47b8834f4913",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c7c9d20c-e7e3-4cf2-b971-bd0ba8c81684",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c7c9d20c-e7e3-4cf2-b971-bd0ba8c81684",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e18aab1e-8566-420c-96a8-48afa6c582e3",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Provide each student with a blank sheet of paper, or have students take out a sheet if they have one.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students one minute to sketch a rough home page for a fictional music playlist app. It should include basic navigation, key sections, a short paragraph, and an image gallery.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize speed and clarity over perfection. Encourage students to use shapes like boxes and lines to represent content. They should avoid details like colors, specific photos, or exact wording. This keeps attention on structure — a core idea in abstraction. If students get stuck, prompt them: \"Where would a user click first? What would they see next?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e18aab1e-8566-420c-96a8-48afa6c582e3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "547f2c2f-52fb-4a1e-a953-749aa4809372",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "547f2c2f-52fb-4a1e-a953-749aa4809372",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ce3e07d-f0d3-4b39-84ca-24c717c57cfb",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students quickly turn to a partner and compare their sketches. Prompt them to identify: what they chose to include (navigation, sections, layout) and what they left out (colors, images, detailed text).",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students get stuck, prompt: \"Did you include colors? Real images? Exact wording?\" This helps them notice they focused on structure."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ce3e07d-f0d3-4b39-84ca-24c717c57cfb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7ae95404-927b-4b28-bbfa-a2a13dc633e6",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7ae95404-927b-4b28-bbfa-a2a13dc633e6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1ac84ea-7176-405f-851c-b99c26d3850f",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If you left out things like colors, exact images, or detailed text, that's exactly right — you're thinking like a designer. In this lesson, you're focusing on how your page is structured — where things go and how a user moves through it. What you just created is your first wireframe — a plan for structure. So what kinds of components do low-fidelity wireframes usually include? Let's take a quick look.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1ac84ea-7176-405f-851c-b99c26d3850f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eb96d5f8-01a6-4490-af8a-3dc0074c513c",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly introduce common building blocks of wireframes.\n- Navigation / Header → how users navigate between pages\n- Image / Video placeholders → where media will go\n- Text blocks → where information will appear\n- Buttons / inputs → how users interact\n\nEmphasize that these are simple, reusable shapes used to quickly plan layout — not final design. Keep this quick, focusing on recognition, not memorization. Let students know they can refer to these components in their Unit Guide at any time. Prompt students to consider what their sketch might be missing based on these components.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Avoid explaining every component in detail. Focus on a few core ones (navigation, sections, media, actions). If time allows, ask: \"Which of these did you already include in your sketch?\" This helps students connect the components to their own work and that they may have already used some of these in their Round 1 sketches; now we're just making them more intentional."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eb96d5f8-01a6-4490-af8a-3dc0074c513c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "22cf124e-67a6-4e6a-a7be-b00650dfd51b",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "22cf124e-67a6-4e6a-a7be-b00650dfd51b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "697a966d-770e-412a-938e-7d432036f730",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students compare the wireframe and final webpage and identify what stayed the same and what changed. Prompt them to consider which parts of the finished webpage come from structure and which come from visual design.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students one minute to sketch an About page for the same app. Prompt them to use the wireframe components from the previous slide to make their layout clearer and more structured. Remind them they can refer to the components in their Unit Guide if needed. Encourage students to think about how users would move between this page and their homepage — designers look for consistency across pages to improve navigation and flow.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that they are focusing on structure and layout, not visual design — this continues the practice of abstraction (ignoring details that don't affect structure) and decomposition (breaking each page into functional sections)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "697a966d-770e-412a-938e-7d432036f730",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "19dd86b8-e08c-4371-b27c-5177b0cb0de6",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "19dd86b8-e08c-4371-b27c-5177b0cb0de6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "66e6411a-ff9e-4f59-8249-51d3e66a6d2b",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students briefly compare their own Round 1 and Round 2 sketches (or compare with a partner). Prompt them to notice what became clearer or more organized and where they used wireframe components more intentionally.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Connect students' experience from the sketch activities to choices that UX Designers make. Explain that when students sketched their pages, they were making decisions about structure and how users move through the app — not focusing on colors or visuals. Emphasize that this is exactly what designers do when creating wireframes: planning structure first so the final product works well before any styling or tools are applied.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *abstraction*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "66e6411a-ff9e-4f59-8249-51d3e66a6d2b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cc9b1b5f-ac2e-4197-9f91-8d85687f602f",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students create low-fidelity wireframes for multiple pages of an app, focusing on clear structure and how users move between pages.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of two.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Wireframe an App Idea "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cc9b1b5f-ac2e-4197-9f91-8d85687f602f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "609566aa-412b-4a5b-bc76-d1c976171ebf",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You're now the UX Designers planning your app. Your job is to decide the structure: how pages are organized and how users move through them. Make your wireframes clear and easy to understand so someone else, or AI, could build your app from them. ",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "609566aa-412b-4a5b-bc76-d1c976171ebf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6db2647b-bb3a-4c40-bb4a-828ab93ec343",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 and 2.\n- **Level 1:** Students create two wireframes each for a chosen a web app idea in Sketch Lab.\n- **Level 2:** Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students have chosen a clear app idea and are creating multiple pages for it. Look for clear navigation between pages and logical connections between sections. Prompt students to think about user flow: \"How would a user move from one page to another?\" or \"What would they click first, then next?\" If groups struggle, remind them that AI can help later with visuals or content, but they are responsible for designing how the app works.",
        "progression_name": "Wireframe an App Idea"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6db2647b-bb3a-4c40-bb4a-828ab93ec343",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9cc2c5b4-2e4b-467e-acb1-f28329728434",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize clear structure and connections between pages. Consistent navigation helps users understand how the app works and reinforces pattern recognition. \n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students' wireframes in Sketch Lab to formatively assess student progress on lesson objectives. Check whether students can:\n- Create a low-fidelity wireframe for their web app's pages.\n- Map a user journey across pages in their wireframes.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9cc2c5b4-2e4b-467e-acb1-f28329728434",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "163c4a8f-af20-4899-ad26-11d6402140b9",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "163c4a8f-af20-4899-ad26-11d6402140b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "20944bc9-c030-4512-9af6-5f8a87789789",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have pairs share their wireframes and user flow with another pair. Students place sticky-note feedback using the sentence starters. Emphasize that feedback should focus on clarity, navigation, and how easily a user can move through the app. When finished, give students a brief moment to reflect on their feedback and consider what our AI tool would need to understand their wireframes clearly.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen to the feedback being exchanged. Ensure students are giving specific feedback (e.g., “I’m not sure how a user gets from this page to the next”). Prompt students to focus on user flow and clarity, not visual details.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use peer feedback to formatively assess student progress on lesson objectives. Check whether students can:\n- Evaluate and explain how clearly a wireframe communicates user flow.\n- Give and receive peer feedback on wireframe clarity."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "20944bc9-c030-4512-9af6-5f8a87789789",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d69944e7-d700-4d7b-acc9-dbf4f6c77a3b",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d69944e7-d700-4d7b-acc9-dbf4f6c77a3b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07b03981-2fef-452c-9def-30c658c51a04"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b6517664-2351-4b1e-a5a6-4bfb349aac75",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students respond to the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide. Give them time to write independently, then invite a few volunteers to share. As students respond, press them to explain why sketching first improves clarity and structure, not just that it is faster or easier, and guide them to connect their answers to planning layout, user flow, and making their designs easier for others to understand.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students give vague answers (e.g., “it’s easier”), prompt with questions like, “What specifically becomes easier?” or “How does sketching help someone else understand your design?” to push for more precise reasoning.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b6517664-2351-4b1e-a5a6-4bfb349aac75",
        "lesson_activity.key": "88076b3f-6d25-4c39-869e-765a3081fcff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "efb14290-02d0-4a4b-aeb1-032b8940e6be",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "efb14290-02d0-4a4b-aeb1-032b8940e6be",
        "lesson_activity.key": "88076b3f-6d25-4c39-869e-765a3081fcff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "763d2d44-761b-4fc2-8347-5bd78d122fd1",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "763d2d44-761b-4fc2-8347-5bd78d122fd1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "88076b3f-6d25-4c39-869e-765a3081fcff"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ba1ddc96-2c14-45ba-a0d0-523932345778",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students distinguish between actions (things happening) and elements (parts of a webpage) as a foundation for understanding interactivity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which of these do you think are actions? Which are parts of the page?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should differentiate between actions and parts of a web page to build a foundation for interactivity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that all the items identified as actions are called events, and all the parts of the page are elements. Connect this back to what they just sorted so it feels like naming their ideas, not introducing new content.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What is one action you wish a web page you have built could have done?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that their previous webpages included parts of a webpage but did not respond to user actions. They begin to distinguish between an action (event) and what the page does in response, setting up the need for interactivity.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students may describe outcomes (\"show a message\") instead of events (\"click a button\"). If needed, guide them to name the action first, then what the page should do in response."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ba1ddc96-2c14-45ba-a0d0-523932345778",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c2f2cfd2-a7db-4dac-9ea4-b7ae19e884ac"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "17195e17-4587-4f70-8d05-485c019071db",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "17195e17-4587-4f70-8d05-485c019071db",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c2f2cfd2-a7db-4dac-9ea4-b7ae19e884ac"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d8102bb6-0935-4345-bfd4-3238d183d6cc",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You have been designing web pages. Think about the pages you built. Your pages had elements, but could they respond to events or do any of the things we just listed? In previous lessons, you focused on designing what a page looks like. Now, you will learn how to make your pages interactive.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d8102bb6-0935-4345-bfd4-3238d183d6cc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c2f2cfd2-a7db-4dac-9ea4-b7ae19e884ac"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa347537-df7c-48ee-a6ca-f521c6bec382",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa347537-df7c-48ee-a6ca-f521c6bec382",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c2f2cfd2-a7db-4dac-9ea4-b7ae19e884ac"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "531cfd36-f87d-4b23-ac91-a0f8ae42e646",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand the DOM is what allows parts of the page to be changed.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Foundations of AI Programming (JavaScript) Unit Guide and the Interactivity in Web Apps activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to walk through the annotated web page and point out specific parts such as the button, text, image, and input. Remind students that these are the parts they identified earlier in the warm-up. Briefly point to the diagram on the right to show that these parts can be grouped and organized within the page, but don't go into detail about the structure. Explain that when something happens on a page, like a click or typing, that is an event, and events happen to these parts. Emphasize that when events happen, these parts of the page can change.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "DOM Introduction",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus on the structure diagram and assume they need to understand how everything is organized. Keep the focus on the idea that a webpage is made of parts that can change. Treat the diagram as a quick visual reference, not something students need to analyze in detail."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "531cfd36-f87d-4b23-ac91-a0f8ae42e646",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eba45916-424e-4d8d-8d63-5530e09ee70c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eba45916-424e-4d8d-8d63-5530e09ee70c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b94b5984-b43a-42d3-8d1c-9d7d24a2db4a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *Document Object Model (DOM)*. Note that something (code) has to tell the page what to change, and that they will look at what causes those changes next.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus on the term \"object\" or get confused by technical language. Keep the explanation grounded in what they can see: \"The DOM is what lets the page change\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b94b5984-b43a-42d3-8d1c-9d7d24a2db4a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d7f3575e-a466-4346-9eb5-19291d8c9966",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d7f3575e-a466-4346-9eb5-19291d8c9966",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6073d8c-eb21-4cd5-910d-0310e569b6ff",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly explain that the structural parts students just identified — headings, buttons, images, navigation — do double duty. They organize the page visually for sighted users, and they help screen reader users navigate the page without seeing it. Emphasize that a heading is not just styled text; it functions as a landmark that screen readers can jump to."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6073d8c-eb21-4cd5-910d-0310e569b6ff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "403eef33-2f50-4e8b-9956-ce9e72ce405b",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When you build a webpage, you're not just deciding what it looks like. You're deciding how someone gets around it. Sighted users scroll and scan. Screen reader users jump from heading to heading, or button to button, to figure out what's on a page. If your headings are just big text, a screen reader user has no map. If they're real headings, the page has a structure they can navigate.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "403eef33-2f50-4e8b-9956-ce9e72ce405b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "728f4937-81af-477b-8066-ec0c3edbbcd9",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that when something changes on a webpage, it doesn't happen automatically—there is code that tells the page what to do. That code is called JavaScript. Explain that JavaScript listens for user actions (events) and tells the page how to respond. Let students know this is the language they will use to make their web apps interactive.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may think the user action itself (\"I clicked\") causes the change. Reinforce that something in the page is \"watching\" for that action and telling the page what to do. Keep the explanation simple; focus on the idea that JavaScript is what makes the page respond, not how the code works."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "728f4937-81af-477b-8066-ec0c3edbbcd9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76e98b7c-a8aa-4175-a109-7895451f9121",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76e98b7c-a8aa-4175-a109-7895451f9121",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "47750bb4-c4c0-4da6-9302-cd28bbbb0e15",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain how interactivity is implemented. Ask students what they notice changed, then focus on the middle steps to explain how the change happens; emphasizing that JavaScript is the middle step, connecting the user action to what changes on the page.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 1. Explain that the page has many parts, but it does not respond to events yet. Students explore the page and identify its parts in their activity guide, then decide which parts could change. Students choose one interaction to make work and use AI Tutor to make that change happen.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who can identify multiple parts of the page and clearly describe what they want to change. Ask: \"What part are you focusing on\" or \"What do you want the page to do?\" or \"What happens right now when you select a button?\"",
        "progression_name": "Static vs Interactive Web App",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students may try to make multiple parts work at once. Encourage them to focus on one interaction and get it working first. If students are stuck, prompt them to describe what the user does and what should change on the page before using AI."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "47750bb4-c4c0-4da6-9302-cd28bbbb0e15",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2802e5a7-d1ac-4876-8ca5-2f27850d307c",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2802e5a7-d1ac-4876-8ca5-2f27850d307c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1f3fb09c-c4fb-43a3-9315-e9c3d74cc5e1",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What made the page change?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students may say \"the button,\" \"clicking,\" or \"the AI.\" Guide students to recognize that the page changed because code made it respond. Introduce that this code is JavaScript, which allows the page to update. Emphasize that these updates happen in the DOM, which is what allows parts of the page to change.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus on the action (\"I clicked\") or the tool (\"the AI\") instead of the underlying cause. Acknowledge their responses, then help them connect the idea that code made the page respond. Avoid going into detail about how the code works—focus on the idea that JavaScript enables the change."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1f3fb09c-c4fb-43a3-9315-e9c3d74cc5e1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7077c6b-051c-4c09-870f-e7d71802036d",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7077c6b-051c-4c09-870f-e7d71802036d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "68f27f66-248d-47b9-b841-280a809be700",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize that interactivity affects user experience and that some interactions work better than others.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly explain that interactivity means a page responds when a user does something, like clicking, typing, or other events. Keep the explanation short and grounded in examples students have already experienced.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Trade-Offs in Design"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "68f27f66-248d-47b9-b841-280a809be700",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1d187a4-5c63-4da2-b3bc-6a2260f4f392",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You just made a page interactive. But here's the next question . . . does every interaction automatically make a page better? Let's test that. You're going to try two versions of an app that both have interaction and compare how easy they are to use.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may assume that \"more interaction = better.\" Do not correct this immediately. Let students test this idea in the next activity so they can experience the difference themselves."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1d187a4-5c63-4da2-b3bc-6a2260f4f392",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "259fda09-2eca-44bd-8910-fe5a8f904f87",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "259fda09-2eca-44bd-8910-fe5a8f904f87",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a11870eb-a318-4aaf-8770-a041ad45ab2a",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 5.\n- Level 2: Students read about interactivity in web apps and accessibility.\n- Level 3: Students experience an app that has a lot of frustrating interactivity.\n- Level 4: Students experience an app of the same purpose that has a much better user experience.\n- Level 5: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Emphasize that their goal is to compare the two experiences and think about how the design affects the user. Look for students who are able to describe what is frustrating in Level 3 and what works better in Level 4. Ask: \"What made this version harder to use\", \"What made the other version easier?\", or \"What changed for the user?\"",
        "progression_name": "Document Design Flaws"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a11870eb-a318-4aaf-8770-a041ad45ab2a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e0a2c263-31f0-4272-b0a2-67cc35895b83",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus on surface features (colors, layout) instead of interaction. Guide them to focus on what happens when they click, type, or try to complete a task. Encourage students to complete the task in each app rather than just clicking around so they fully experience the frustration or improvement."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e0a2c263-31f0-4272-b0a2-67cc35895b83",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "14b75b74-3dfd-45dd-ade5-c37ab83c10cc",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "14b75b74-3dfd-45dd-ade5-c37ab83c10cc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "001d84a1-f509-4a41-9fb8-69d3e037e6d6",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which version was better? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that not all interactions improve the user experience and begin to connect interaction to design. Guide students to understand that interactivity is not just about making things happen, but about making things happen in a way that helps the user.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may give general answers like \"this one was better\" without explaining why. Prompt them to describe specific interactions (what happened when they clicked or typed) and how those interactions affected their experience. If students focus only on visual appearance, redirect them to how the app responded to their actions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "001d84a1-f509-4a41-9fb8-69d3e037e6d6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3f12e29e-c177-44be-8121-c104ae6211c7",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3f12e29e-c177-44be-8121-c104ae6211c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f064fe9-aaeb-4701-bec1-f762ff2924cb",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Quick Design Sprint section of their activity guide. Ensure they choose a specific user to focus on. Students can either draw the app and label their improvements or write a short description of how they would improve it for that user. If students are unsure who to choose, provide examples such as someone using it quickly, someone who gets confused easily, or someone using it on a small screen.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may suggest changes that are not connected to a specific user (e.g., \"make it better\" or \"add more features\"). Prompt them to explain who their change is helping and why. If needed, ask: \"How does this help your user?\" or \"What problem are you solving for them?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f064fe9-aaeb-4701-bec1-f762ff2924cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb9fb977-09ab-4e6e-ba15-5aa449a4cd98",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb9fb977-09ab-4e6e-ba15-5aa449a4cd98",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d9a85340-8991-4a54-95da-46a6bd5ccf16",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Would your change make anything harder for someone else?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that improving an app for one user can sometimes create challenges for others. Guide students to notice that design decisions often involve trade-offs, where making something better in one way may make it harder in another.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may assume their change is only beneficial and have difficulty identifying downsides. Prompt them to think about different users by asking, \"Who might find this harder to use?\" or \"What could be confusing about this change?\" If needed, provide examples to model how one improvement can create a new challenge."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d9a85340-8991-4a54-95da-46a6bd5ccf16",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2b5b9ea2-d03d-4662-ac4a-e13ba34ef144"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f07747c4-dead-41d7-a397-0b3ed9942ed5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to explain how a web app responds to a user by describing an event and what changes on the page. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f07747c4-dead-41d7-a397-0b3ed9942ed5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5017f33b-4baf-42d4-9f6c-79a046910c17"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "56aed80d-c06d-4f46-a742-feae750973ec",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students connect the role of a front-end developer to building interactivity on a webpage and recognize JavaScript as the tool that makes interactions happen.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> Do This: Briefly spotlight the role of a Front-End Developer. Explain that this role is responsible for what users see and how a webpage responds when they interact with it.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Invite students to name a website or app where something responds instantly (e.g., a button click, animation, or form). Ask, \"What do you think the developer had to decide to make that interaction work?\" This helps students connect the role to real interactions they use every day."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "56aed80d-c06d-4f46-a742-feae750973ec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c18dbd4d-fd41-469a-9cd8-8aa9cd0277ec"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b088011-4eaa-417f-ac15-9fec40cf3cc6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b088011-4eaa-417f-ac15-9fec40cf3cc6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c18dbd4d-fd41-469a-9cd8-8aa9cd0277ec"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "48483e62-47e1-4a0e-af73-d1e81e68bb8c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *A front-end developer uses JavaScript to make a button respond to a click. If that developer used AI to build a “Like” button, what would they need to tell the AI to make it work?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that a developer must clearly describe both the user action and what should happen in response when using AI to build an interaction. They begin to notice that something must detect the action and trigger the response, rather than the change happening automatically. This sets up the idea that interactions follow a structured process, which will be explored through event listeners and event handlers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "48483e62-47e1-4a0e-af73-d1e81e68bb8c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c18dbd4d-fd41-469a-9cd8-8aa9cd0277ec"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "acf67e9e-0077-47d1-b647-f7bee406f559",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand how webpages respond to user actions by connecting the roles of event listener (detect) and event handler (respond).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Foundations of AI Programming (JavaScript) Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *event listener*. Emphasize that it is attached to an element and waits for a specific user action (click, hover, key press).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *event handler*. Emphasize that this is the response — what actually happens after the event.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students quickly form small groups of three to four and assign one role per person: Element, User, Listener, and Handler. Emphasize that each role represents a part of what happens behind the scenes when someone interacts with a web page. Give students about 30–60 seconds to decide who will take on each role, and prompt them to read their role name aloud within their group so everyone is clear.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As groups assign roles, circulate to ensure each group has all roles covered and to clarify confusion, especially between the Event Listener and Event Handler. If needed, briefly restate that the listener waits for the action and the handler decides what happens next.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have each group decide on a simple interaction such as a tap, clap, or press. Once they've chosen their interaction, direct them to say the three planning statements out loud together within their group: what their event is, what the listener is waiting for, and what the handler will do. Emphasize that this is a quick call-and-response style step and should take no more than 1–2 minutes. The goal is for every group to clearly state their plan before acting, not to write anything down or overthink it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As groups plan, circulate to ensure each group has selected a clear interaction and is actually saying their responses out loud. If a group is quiet or vague, prompt them to say their plan together and be more specific about what will happen.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Intro to Event Handling",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage groups to keep interactions simple (tap, clap, press) so the focus stays on the sequence, not the scenario."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "acf67e9e-0077-47d1-b647-f7bee406f559",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c8e5362-9512-4047-b17d-4c8b153759d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bc66db83-7ad8-4bc4-a277-eeb4391a53a5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bc66db83-7ad8-4bc4-a277-eeb4391a53a5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c8e5362-9512-4047-b17d-4c8b153759d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9bb454e6-b6a4-4f75-9687-3d63b332a412",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct them to act it out using the four-step sequence: the user performs the action, the listener announces that the event was detected and calls \"Run,\" and the handler performs the response. Emphasize that the handler cannot act until the listener calls it, reinforcing the idea that something must detect the event before code runs. Give groups a minute or two to run through the sequence at least once, and encourage them to repeat it with a different response if they finish quickly.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students act out the sequence, circulate to ensure groups are following the correct order and using the language of \"event,\" \"listener,\" and \"handler\" appropriately. If a group skips a step or has the handler acting too early, pause them and ask which step might be missing. Pay close attention to confusion between the listener and handler roles, and reinforce that the listener detects and triggers while the handler performs the response.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Make sure groups explicitly say \"Run\" before the handler acts. This step represents the moment code executes and is critical for understanding how event handling works.\n\nIf students confuse the listener and handler roles, restate that the listener detects and triggers, while the handler performs the action."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9bb454e6-b6a4-4f75-9687-3d63b332a412",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c8e5362-9512-4047-b17d-4c8b153759d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f5d0fb51-019c-4a7c-80b9-d3f24da482a5",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f5d0fb51-019c-4a7c-80b9-d3f24da482a5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c8e5362-9512-4047-b17d-4c8b153759d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "51a581cb-caa2-4bb9-b660-363d49e1bd85",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Who detected the event?*\n- *Who triggered the action?*\n- *Who performed the action?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that different parts of the system have distinct roles in making an interaction happen. The event listener is responsible for detecting when a user action occurs, but it does not perform the action itself—it triggers the next step. The event handler is what actually runs and produces the visible change on the page. Through this discussion, guide students to understand the sequence: a user action (event) is detected by a listener, which then calls the handler to perform the response. This reinforces that interactions do not happen automatically—there is a structured process that connects user input to changes on the screen, which will later map directly to how event listeners and event handlers work in code."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "51a581cb-caa2-4bb9-b660-363d49e1bd85",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c8e5362-9512-4047-b17d-4c8b153759d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2b141eae-1dea-4ece-9222-85aaee6967c3",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will apply the Select → Listen → Respond model to build interactive features, use AI to generate event-based code, and verify that their code correctly targets elements and updates the page.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to explain that what students just acted out is how event handling works on a web page:\n- The first step is interacts. The user interacts with an element, like clicking a button. Nothing changes yet — this is just the action the user takes.\n- The second step is listen. The page listens for that action. This is the event listener — it waits for a specific event, like a click, and detects when it happens.\n- The final step is respond. The event listener calls the event handler, and the event handler runs the code. This is where something actually changes on the page, like text updating or an image appearing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this code as representing the same three-step process students just acted out for how a web page responds to a user action.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that this line of code selects an element on the page (like the button).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that this line of code adds an event listener. It tells the page what event to listen for — so in this case, a click.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that this line of code is the event handler, which responds to the event. The code that runs and updates the page (text, image, color, etc.).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that AI-generated code may look correct but still not work, and model how to question and verify whether the code actually matches the event or selected element from the prompt. (especially IDs).",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Make a Button Respond"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2b141eae-1dea-4ece-9222-85aaee6967c3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c8e5362-9512-4047-b17d-4c8b153759d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "535a3ffa-46e5-4f08-9365-e59bc99bbbfd",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI sounds confident and can generate code that looks correct, but it doesn’t actually know if it will work on your specific page. It is making a best guess based on patterns, so when we see something like this ID \"buttonID,\" we have to stop and ask: does an element with this ID actually exist in our web page, because if it doesn't, nothing will happen even though the code looks right.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "535a3ffa-46e5-4f08-9365-e59bc99bbbfd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c8e5362-9512-4047-b17d-4c8b153759d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b284c9bc-9ebb-4ea3-9a00-7714d2717e80",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the term context blindness as the reason AI's code can look completely correct but still fail. Explain that AI generates code based on patterns it has seen in other pages, not on what is actually in the project. The ID it guesses might exist somewhere on the internet, just not in the project."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b284c9bc-9ebb-4ea3-9a00-7714d2717e80",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c8e5362-9512-4047-b17d-4c8b153759d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "05aadb78-b218-4f19-8c3a-0851e8156e04",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When AI gives you code that doesn't work, it's not because AI is broken. It's because AI doesn't know what's in your page. It's guessing based on patterns from code it has seen before. That guess is usually plausible — it looks right — but plausible isn't the same as correct. This kind of mistake has a name: context blindness. AI is blind to your context. That's why showing AI your element, describing it in detail, and asking it to confirm matter so much. You're filling in the context AI can't see.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "05aadb78-b218-4f19-8c3a-0851e8156e04",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c8e5362-9512-4047-b17d-4c8b153759d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f1f6af6a-0c0f-4181-a06d-df90847fce48",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to introduce each steps students can use to create and debug AI-generated code. As each tip appears, connect it to how it helps improve their prompt:\n- First, show the element. Emphasize that including a screenshot helps AI understand what part of the page the student is working with.\n- Next, describe the element in detail. Highlight that students should include specific details like text, color, or position so AI can correctly identify it.\n- Finally, confirm the element. Model asking AI to verify which element it's selecting to make sure it's targeting the correct one."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f1f6af6a-0c0f-4181-a06d-df90847fce48",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c8e5362-9512-4047-b17d-4c8b153759d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf10c38b-9100-4c5e-a1d8-9ab3a6f6d5db",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When your button isn't working, don't just guess! Be specific. First, show the element so the AI can see what you're working with. Then describe the element in detail — what it says, what it looks like, where it is. And if it's still not working, ask AI to confirm which element it's selecting, because sometimes it's targeting the wrong one.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf10c38b-9100-4c5e-a1d8-9ab3a6f6d5db",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c8e5362-9512-4047-b17d-4c8b153759d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c567694-7ee8-448a-bd89-eff86635f31d",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you're going to apply what you learned by acting like a front-end developer—your job is to make a webpage respond to user actions by selecting elements, adding event listeners, and writing event handlers. You can use AI to help generate code, but you also need to test it and make sure it actually works when you interact with the page.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c567694-7ee8-448a-bd89-eff86635f31d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c8e5362-9512-4047-b17d-4c8b153759d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5199fe5-99e2-4c68-8c8b-84e7b8fbcd6e",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 7, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 6.\n- Level 1: Predict and trace what the code will do when a button is clicked.\n- Level 2: Use AI to generate code for one button interaction.\n- Level 3: Write code yourself to add a second button interaction.\n- Level 4: Conduct a day-in-the-life interview with a front-end developer chatbot.\n- Level 5: Build an interactive app based on a chosen scenario.\n- Level 6: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** In Level 1, focus on helping students connect the code to the event handling model. Check that they can identify where the code selects an element, listens for an event, and responds. Ask questions like, \"What event is this listening for?\" and \"What actually changes on the page when you click?\" In Levels 2 and 3, support students in working with AI-generated code. Watch for common issues like nothing happening on click or the wrong element responding. Prompt students with, \"Which element is this code targeting?\" and \"How can you tell if it's the right one?\" In Level 5, push students toward fully working interactions. Encourage them to test each button and verify that every interaction works as expected.",
        "progression_name": "Adding Interactivity"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5199fe5-99e2-4c68-8c8b-84e7b8fbcd6e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c8e5362-9512-4047-b17d-4c8b153759d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "62a91d06-f28d-4ce1-ac72-63d9cfd90b42",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to test frequently after adding each interaction. Remind them that if nothing happens, it's usually because the code is not targeting the correct element or the interaction isn't set up properly."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Levels 1 through 5 to assess whether students can add an event handler to make a button interactive and update page content when a button is clicked. Look for evidence that students can correctly select elements, attach event listeners, and create responses that change text or visuals on the page."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "62a91d06-f28d-4ce1-ac72-63d9cfd90b42",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1c8e5362-9512-4047-b17d-4c8b153759d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b654f131-3d4c-41c9-8bad-10d9575eeec2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to explain how a web page responds to a click by describing both what the user does and what changes on the page. Prompt students to be specific about what should happen when a button is clicked. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What part of being a Front-End Developer did you like?*\n- *What part was frustrating?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should reflect on their experience of doing Front-End Developer work and start forming personal opinions about whether the role fits them. Listen for specific responses — what they liked or didn't like about the actual work, not just the activity. Students might mention the satisfaction of seeing something they built respond to a click, the creative freedom of picking content, the frustration of debugging AI-generated code, or the cognitive load of switching between writing prompts and reading code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b654f131-3d4c-41c9-8bad-10d9575eeec2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8f7f6fb-4e93-4f7b-ae51-73231326601d"
      }
    }
  ],
